Until document is completed, use '\qtwopages \shipout\vbox{}' instead of '\quire{...}'

=======================================================================================
DESIGN DECISIONS:

Added UTF-8 support using web2w-0.4 from CTAN.
(Leaved out '#define round' from ctex.patch because round() in C behaves identically.
Seems this was introduced because Martin Ruckert used MinGW, where round(), lround()
and llround() fail to correctly round values that sit exactly one epsilon away from
the halfway point.)

Open log-file in graphical text editor and delete or add excess or missing
newlines there. When editor detects that log-file changed on disk, it shows
"reload" button - just press it.

In formats based on тех.tex, \\ is a dedicated control sequence
(in contrast to plain.tex).

For strut-based code (including tables) use uppercase font commands (e.g.,
TENRM). (In \headline also use uppercase font commands, because \makeheadline
uses \vbox to <height of strut>.) I make fonts named with uppercase letters,
which are the same as fonts named with lowercase letters, except that height of
uppercase accented characters is the same as height of uppercase non-accented
characters.

In Metafont there are parameter files, driver files and program files (p.304 in
MFbook). I took parameter files for all CM-fonts (except math fonts), only
naming them differently and using different driver files in them, and simply
included in the driver files required program files from LH-fonts. I also used
sauter extension to create some such fonts in sizes which are missing in
CM-fonts.

After being built, TeX and MF must be configured by running 'make' in mytex/
(done automatically).

Only PK fonts are used (no font maps).

PK fonts are automatically generated by MakePK in mytex/dvips

MakePK generates pk files for ./ fonts on each run, because mf mode in current
run may be different from mf mode in previous run (tex- vs. tex+, dvi- vs. dvi+)
and PK-file contains no information about the MF mode from which it was obtained.
Also, if you are editing a character, you want to see new version of it on each run.

Information about paper size, horizontal and vertical origins is contained in DVI
file comment and in mytex/dvips this information is extracted and passed to dvips
via -T and -O options.

Start mf file with %! line if you need to set geometry, for example:
  %! pdfpagewidth=210mm pdfpageheight=297mm pdfhorigin=4.1mm pdfvorigin=20mm
(the geometry is added to GF comment in mf/comment.ch; GF comment
is passed as-is to DVI comment by gftodvi)

Use \pdfhorigin and \pdfvorigin to set margins, \pdfpagewidth and \pdfpageheight
to set paper size. (\pdfpagewidth, \pdfpageheight, \pdfhorigin and \pdfvorigin
are stored in comment of DVI file) Default \pdfhorigin and \pdfvorigin for
letter paper are 1in; to use default \hsize and \vsize for a4 paper they are
changed as follows:
  (210-6.5*25.4)/2=22.45mm
  (297-9*25.4)/2=34.2mm

PS is used instead of PDF because PS format allows to split document into pages,
leaving the rest of the document intact. And page splitting is critical, because
we can not generate only necessary pages when we convert from DVI - we need to
actually see all the pages to be able to choose which pages to print, which
means that we must generate the whole document and open it in viewer - page
splitting will be done at a later stage by viewer.

For printing use only viewer which uses lpr program for printing (there are
viewers that connect to cups socket directly). This allows to override lpr with
ad-hoc program which sends the PS data directly to printer (after wrapping it in
PJL commands).
